1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,494
Here's a receipt an early
version of my own build wrote.

2
00:00:02,534 --> 00:00:07,696
An AI shopping agent bought $42.50 of
groceries. Every line is filled in.

3
00:00:07,926 --> 00:00:09,220
Every field is well-formed.

4
00:00:09,240 --> 00:00:11,789
And it says the purchase
was authorized for Morgan.

5
00:00:11,829 --> 00:00:16,178
Morgan never saw it. The agent belonged to
somebody else, a cardholder called Devon.

6
00:00:16,218 --> 00:00:18,123
So how does a receipt
like that get written?

7
00:00:18,163 --> 00:00:21,135
And what would you check to
make sure yours never does?

8
00:00:21,175 --> 00:00:24,474
Hi, this is Tanmay Kulkarni,
in for Humanitarians AI.

9
00:00:24,835 --> 00:00:26,555
This one's about Mastercard Agent Pay,

10
00:00:26,575 --> 00:00:30,028
the system that lets an AI agent
pay on a cardholder's behalf.

11
00:00:30,068 --> 00:00:32,735
I built a working version of its
checks using only public information,

12
00:00:32,755 --> 00:00:35,827
and it's yours to run:
82 tests, all passing.

13
00:00:36,255 --> 00:00:37,395
For purchases like these,

14
00:00:37,415 --> 00:00:40,567
Mastercard has described a
record called Verifiable Intent,

15
00:00:40,607 --> 00:00:43,783
"a tamper-resistant record
of what a user authorized."

16
00:00:43,949 --> 00:00:46,773
Every completed purchase
in my build writes one.

17
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:49,600
So here's how I read it now. Backwards.

18
00:00:49,620 --> 00:00:53,062
Start at the bottom of the receipt,
and for every line, ask one question.

19
00:00:53,102 --> 00:00:55,184
Which step checked this,
before it was written down?

20
00:00:55,224 --> 00:00:56,668
A line with an answer is earned.

21
00:00:56,708 --> 00:00:59,044
A line without one is
just being carried along.

22
00:00:59,084 --> 00:01:01,695
Mastercard asks the same
thing, in its own words.

23
00:01:01,735 --> 00:01:05,818
"How do we know an agent is doing
exactly what we asked, and nothing more?"

24
00:01:05,858 --> 00:01:09,438
The agent line first. Mastercard's
framework "begins by registering

25
00:01:09,458 --> 00:01:13,164
and verifying AI agents before
they are permitted to transact."

26
00:01:13,484 --> 00:01:15,119
In my build, that's two separate checks.

27
00:01:15,159 --> 00:01:18,058
An agent the network has never
seen and an agent whose credentials

28
00:01:18,078 --> 00:01:21,885
just failed are two different problems,
with two different answers.

29
00:01:21,925 --> 00:01:23,101
This line is earned.

30
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:26,175
Now the person line, the
one on that first receipt.

31
00:01:26,215 --> 00:01:29,688
An agent can be registered and
verified, and still not be yours.

32
00:01:29,728 --> 00:01:31,300
In the early version, nothing compared who

33
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:34,236
the agent actually belongs to with
who it said it was buying for.

34
00:01:34,276 --> 00:01:36,409
So Devon's agent could buy "for Morgan."

35
00:01:36,449 --> 00:01:38,983
And because Morgan had never
set any rules for that agent,

36
00:01:39,023 --> 00:01:41,328
it looked like an ordinary
new kind of purchase.

37
00:01:41,368 --> 00:01:45,275
So it went to the gate that decides
new cases, the demo's gate said yes,

38
00:01:45,315 --> 00:01:47,180
and the receipt said Morgan approved it.

39
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:48,799
The second review round caught that.

40
00:01:48,839 --> 00:01:51,955
Now one step asks first: does
this agent belong to this person?

41
00:01:51,995 --> 00:01:54,930
If it doesn't, the purchase stops,
before anything else runs.

42
00:01:54,970 --> 00:01:59,558
Mastercard says its record "confirms the
cardholder authorizing the AI agent."

43
00:01:59,698 --> 00:02:02,698
In my build, this is the
step that earns that line.

44
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,007
The category line, and I found
this one while making this video.

45
00:02:06,047 --> 00:02:10,299
Devon set a rule: groceries,
up to $150, until the end of 2026.

46
00:02:11,024 --> 00:02:12,800
A grocery order dated
after that should come back

47
00:02:12,820 --> 00:02:14,649
to Devon for a decision, and it did.

48
00:02:14,689 --> 00:02:16,200
But write the category with a capital H,

49
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:18,572
and the lookup missed
Devon's rule completely.

50
00:02:18,612 --> 00:02:20,580
It treated it as a category
Devon had never set,

51
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:25,032
sent it to that same gate, and the gate
said yes. The receipt looked perfect.

52
00:02:25,072 --> 00:02:28,013
So now every category is
compared in one spelling.

53
00:02:28,212 --> 00:02:30,528
Capitals and spaces don't matter anymore,

54
00:02:30,568 --> 00:02:33,568
and that order comes back
to Devon, like it should.

55
00:02:33,841 --> 00:02:36,061
What I didn't do is teach
it that "groceries" means

56
00:02:36,081 --> 00:02:38,317
the same thing as "household staples."

57
00:02:38,856 --> 00:02:42,410
That would mean inventing a category
list Mastercard hasn't published.

58
00:02:42,450 --> 00:02:44,361
So a different word still
counts as a new category,

59
00:02:44,381 --> 00:02:47,558
and the build says so, in a
test, instead of guessing.

60
00:02:48,433 --> 00:02:51,873
Amount and date. The first review
round made sure those are a real number

61
00:02:51,893 --> 00:02:54,599
and a real date before
anything compares them.

62
00:02:54,788 --> 00:02:56,973
A value that isn't a
number, or minus $500,

63
00:02:56,993 --> 00:02:59,653
used to slip through as an ordinary price.

64
00:02:59,673 --> 00:03:01,555
Now they're stopped at the door.

65
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:05,920
The path line says how the purchase was
approved: within the limits Devon set,

66
00:03:05,940 --> 00:03:09,646
or by the authorization gate, for
the one case Devon never covered.

67
00:03:09,686 --> 00:03:13,244
And that gate ships with no
built-in rule at all, on purpose.

68
00:03:13,284 --> 00:03:16,853
Mastercard hasn't said what should
happen when a consumer never set a rule,

69
00:03:16,893 --> 00:03:21,246
so the build asks whoever runs it to
decide, instead of deciding for them.

70
00:03:21,408 --> 00:03:23,160
Here's what reading backwards taught me.

71
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:24,953
A seal protects a record
after it's written.

72
00:03:24,993 --> 00:03:27,625
It can show that nobody
changed it, and who wrote it.

73
00:03:27,665 --> 00:03:31,148
It can't make the lines true. Mastercard
describes its record as tamper-resistant,

74
00:03:31,168 --> 00:03:32,639
with cryptographic proof.

75
00:03:33,066 --> 00:03:34,808
Mine has no cryptography
at all, on purpose,

76
00:03:34,828 --> 00:03:36,912
because the signing scheme isn't public.

77
00:03:36,952 --> 00:03:40,728
But whatever the real one uses, a seal
can only seal what the pipeline hands it.

78
00:03:40,768 --> 00:03:43,960
The truth of every line comes
from the checks before it.

79
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:45,788
Which leaves one line. Merchant.

80
00:03:45,828 --> 00:03:48,641
Mastercard says a token can
be restricted by merchant.

81
00:03:48,681 --> 00:03:51,000
It hasn't published how that
restriction gets checked.

82
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:54,748
So my build carries the merchant onto the
receipt, and checks it against nothing.

83
00:03:54,788 --> 00:03:58,346
Buy running shoes at a grocery store,
and the receipt says the grocery store.

84
00:03:58,386 --> 00:04:01,194
That's the build telling you exactly
where the public record stops,

85
00:04:01,234 --> 00:04:03,940
and exactly which line
you're taking on trust.

86
00:04:04,258 --> 00:04:05,960
So try it on something you've built.

87
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,594
Take one record your system writes:
a receipt, an approval, a line in a log.

88
00:04:09,634 --> 00:04:10,938
Go through it from the bottom to the top,

89
00:04:10,958 --> 00:04:13,808
and next to every line, write
down the step that checked it.

90
00:04:13,848 --> 00:04:15,938
For any line with nothing next
to it, either add the check,

91
00:04:15,958 --> 00:04:19,301
or say on the record that it isn't
verified. That's the whole move.

92
00:04:19,341 --> 00:04:22,139
The build's repository is
linked below: 82 tests,

93
00:04:22,179 --> 00:04:25,179
plain Python, and a receipt
you can audit yourself.

94
00:04:25,308 --> 00:04:26,781
Read the receipt backwards.

95
00:04:26,821 --> 00:04:30,303
Tanmay Kulkarni, in for
Humanitarians AI, signing off.
